    214	### E1.10 — Gap-shuffled-zeros: the only valid null for prime-frequency probes of zeta-zero D(s)  [EVS shape]
    215	
    216	S49; `experiments/analytic/zeta_structure/large_n_correlations/large_n_battery_results.md`;
    217	`archive/sessions/session49_focus4_large_n_zeta.md`.
    218	
    219	For any test that Fourier-decomposes the pair-correlation residual
    220	D(s) = R_2_emp(s) − R_2_GUE(s) at frequencies tied to primes
    221	(e.g. f_p = log(p)/L for the Bogomolny-Keating arithmetic
    222	correction), the **uniform-random-frequency null is wrong**: it
    223	produces false-positive p < 0.05 detections even on data with no
    224	arithmetic content beyond local GUE statistics.
    225	
    226	The *correct* null is the **gap-shuffled** sequence: take the
    227	unfolded zeros, compute the gaps, randomly permute them, regenerate
    228	the sequence by cumulative summing the shuffled gaps. Gap-shuffling
    229	preserves the local GUE pair correlation (the gap distribution) but
    230	destroys long-range arithmetic structure beyond nearest-neighbour.
    231	
    232	S49 measurement at N=8000:
    233	
    234	```
    235	Statistic                  Zeta      Gap-shuffled null    z-score
    236	Pearson(D, BK_template)   +0.111     +0.487 ± 0.035       −10.85σ
    237	Phase coherence ⟨cos(φ-π)⟩ +0.544     +0.624 ± 0.036       −2.20σ
    238	```
    239	
    240	Both statistics on real zeta are **below** the gap-shuffled null —
    241	zeta is *more* GUE-like than the matched null, the opposite of what
    242	a real BK arithmetic signature would produce.
