    # Prime Geodesics and the Selberg Trace Formula
    
    ## Prime Geodesic Theorem
    
    For cofinite Fuchsian groups like SL₂(ℤ) acting on the hyperbolic upper half-plane, the prime geodesic theorem counts primitive closed geodesics. The Wikipedia article states: "The analogy between the Selberg trace formula and the explicit formulas of prime number theory leads to the prime geodesic theorem, which counts primitive closed geodesics."
    
    This theorem provides asymptotics for primitive hyperbolic conjugacy classes by length or norm, directly paralleling the prime number theorem.
    
    ## Spectral-Geometric Connection
    
    The trace formula establishes a fundamental duality: "In this case the Selberg trace formula is formally similar to the explicit formulas relating the zeros of the Riemann zeta function to prime numbers, with the zeta zeros corresponding to eigenvalues of the Laplacian, and the primes corresponding to geodesics."
    
    For compact hyperbolic surfaces, the formula sums Laplacian eigenvalues on the left side against a sum over conjugacy classes (closed geodesics) on the right. This expresses how spectral data encodes geometric information about geodesic lengths.
    
    ## Asymptotic Formulas
    
    The article references "Weyl-type asymptotics" for eigenvalues via suitable test functions, yielding distribution laws for Laplacian spectrum. These asymptotic results parallel prime-counting functions in analytic number theory.
    
    ## Computational Applications
    
    The trace formula provides practical tools: "the trace formula and the Selberg zeta function are standard tools in the proof of this theorem and in refinements of its error term." For congruence subgroups, it helps compute dimensions of modular form spaces and trace Hecke operators—historically calculated via the Riemann-Roch theorem.
    
    ## Class Numbers and Fundamental Units
    
    The document does not contain explicit formulas connecting class numbers or fundamental units to prime geodesics on the modular surface. Such connections exist in the broader theory but aren't detailed here.
    
    ## Computational Complexity
    
    The article provides no discussion of computational complexity for evaluating the spectral side of the trace formula.
    
