    2226	
    2227	For Szegedy quantum walks (Szegedy 2004, arxiv quant-ph/0401053) on
    2228	reversible Markov chains over `[1..x]` whose transition graph is
    2229	defined by a simple arithmetic relation, polylog primality extraction
    2230	requires *jointly* (i) classical spectral gap `δ = Ω(1/polylog(x))`
    2231	AND (ii) a single eigenvector of the discriminant matrix with
    2232	primality-localised mass. Three test families demonstrate (i) and (ii)
    2233	are **incompatible** in this setting:
    2234	
    2235	- **Cayley `(Z/NZ)*`:** `δ → 0` as `N^{-c}`, c ≈ 0.87 — Szegedy
    2236	  mixing `~N^{0.41}` is poly, not polylog.
    2237	- **Coprime graph `C_x = ([1..x], gcd=1)`:** `δ = Ω(1)` (asymptotically
    2238	  `δ → 0.4166...`); only the trivial Perron eigenvector carries
    2239	  primality information (stationary mass on primes ≈ `(π²/6)/x`,
    2240	  exactly Mertens density `ζ(2)`).
    2241	- **Divisor graph `D_x`:** `δ = Ω(1)`; high-prime-mass eigenvectors
    2242	  exist but localise on prime-centered divisor clusters (one specific
    2243	  prime `p` + its multiples per eigenvector), not on global primality.
    2244	  Same degree-class probing mechanism as E7.12.
    2245	
    2246	Verified at full sweep: 14 Cayley primes, 8 coprime sizes (x ≤ 1000),
