    | §D13 ATTACK_VECTORS — Subword (factor) complexity p_w(n) of chi_P as a binary infinite word, with topological entropy h_w (cross-domain: symbolic dynamics on prime sequences, Lind-Marcus 1995 *Symbolic Dynamics and Coding* CUP, Cassaigne-Nicolas 2010 "Factor complexity" CANT vol. 135, Morse-Hedlund 1938 *Amer. J. Math.* 60) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether the prime indicator binary word w = chi_P(2)chi_P(3)... has subword complexity p_w(n) := #{distinct length-n factors of w} that deviates from matched-density Bernoulli — the canonical combinatorial-on-words test (no published value for chi_P; Wikipedia "Complexity function"). Promotes the informal CLOSED_PATHS line 181 "Symbolic dynamics — near-random block complexity (S4)" to a precise quantitative measurement with finite-N value, K=20 baselines, and full W-trick cascade. Protocol: sieve to N = 5·10^6 (348,513 primes); five chi_P-derived streams — RAW (chi_P(2..N), L=4,999,999, density 0.0697); ODD (chi_P(2k+1), L=2,499,999, density 0.139); W6 (chi_P(6n+1), L=833,334, density 0.209); W30 (chi_P(30n+1), L=166,667, density 0.261); W210 (chi_P(210n+1), L=23,810, density 0.305). Per stream, n ∈ [1, 22] vectorised rolling-encoding factor count (numpy uint64; cost O(nL), memory O(L)); K=20 random permutations of the stream (B2: preserves density and 1-marginal exactly, kills all >1-gram structure) and K=20 iid Bernoulli matched-density samples (B1). Pre-registered F3 (PRIMES > 3σ from BOTH B1 AND B2 at every n in [n_lo, n_hi] with n_hi ≥ 18, on at least one of {ODD, W6, W30}) HOLDS at z >> 3σ for ODD (max |z_perm| = 277 at n=7), W6 (|z_perm| = 121 at n=8), and W30 (|z_perm| = 25 at n=17). Cascade |z_perm| at peak across streams: RAW 132.7 → ODD 277.1 → W6 120.5 → W30 24.8 → W210 8.4 — clean monotone reduction by ~1.5 orders of magnitude across W ∈ {1, 6, 30, 210}. Ratio p_chi(22) / p_perm_mean(22): RAW 0.018 (98% deficit, parity-driven), ODD 0.216 (78% deficit, mod-3 in odd-stream), W6 0.806 (19% deficit), W30 0.994, W210 1.011 (≈ noise; sign flips above n=17 indicating saturation). Effective topological entropy h_eff(n) := log_2 p_w(n) / n at W=210, n=22: chi 0.6581 vs perm 0.6574 (match to 0.001, i.e. log_2(L)/22 saturation). **Mechanism**: subword complexity counts distinct configurations of primes inside a sliding length-n window; chi_P configurations are constrained by mod-p admissibility for p ≤ n (only one prime can be a multiple of p, except p itself). The W = primorial(p_k) sieve removes those constraints for p ≤ p_k, monotonically erasing the deficit. Same Hardy-Littlewood k-tuple admissibility engine that drives E2.13 Gowers, E2.14 Anderson, E2.16 DPP failure, E2.17 PH. Residual |z_perm| = 8.4 at W=210, n=12 is consistent with `O(1/log N)` HL singular-series tail. **Adds EDGE E2.19**: subword complexity of chi_P deviates ≥ 100σ from Bernoulli matched-density on raw/W6 streams; W=210 W-trick reduces to ≤ 8.4σ at finite L = 2.4·10^4. **38th pseudorandomness measure**, **SIXTH orthogonal HL-detection family** (after E2.13 Gowers, E2.14 Anderson, E2.15 algebraic immunity, E2.16 DPP, E2.17 PH), in a NEW mathematical category (symbolic-dynamics / factor-complexity / topological-entropy). **Closure mode E**: subword complexity at given window length is O(L log L) — no polylog opening; W-trick fingerprint encodes only HL information already known. **Cross-domain ingredient (factor complexity / topological entropy of binary words) performed real work**: first project quantitative use; status in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §5 upgraded PROPOSED → USED E with edge E2.19. Cites E2.13, E2.14, E2.15, E2.16, E2.17, E2.18, E7.7. Cross-domain refs: Cassaigne-Nicolas 2010 "Factor complexity" CANT vol. 135 (Cambridge); Lind-Marcus 1995 *An Introduction to Symbolic Dynamics and Coding* (Cambridge); Morse-Hedlund 1938-40 "Symbolic Dynamics" *Amer. J. Math.* 60. See experiments/dynamical/subword_complexity_chi_p/subword_complexity_chi_p_results.md. | 104 |
    | §G1 ATTACK_VECTORS — Liouville Anderson Lyapunov: spectral signature of Möbius pseudorandomness (cross-domain: Anderson localisation [reused S88] + Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality, Green-Tao 2012 *Annals* 175 = arXiv:0807.1736; Sarnak 2010 IAS lectures; Tao 2016 logarithmic-Chowla = arXiv:1509.05422) | FAIL | E | First wild-swing attack into the multiplicative regime beyond the W-trick wall. Setup identical to S88/E2.14 except potential is the **centered multiplicative encoding** V(n) = lambda(n) ∈ {-1, +1} (mean → 0 by PNT for Liouville, variance = 1 exactly, NOT the {0, 1} encoding S88 used). Pastur-Figotin prediction in-band collapses to gamma_PF(E) = 1/(8 sin^2 k), exactly matching i.i.d. Rademacher ±1 baseline. Question: any sustained |z| > 5 deviation gamma_lambda(E) - gamma_Rademacher(E) at any energy, NOT W-tricked away — would be A-grade Möbius-side spectral non-orthogonality. **Empirical at 51 energies in E ∈ [-2.95, 2.95], three sample sizes: max |z| = 1.78 (N=10^5, 50 seeds, argmax E=-0.236), 2.16 (N=3·10^5, 50 seeds, argmax +0.118), 2.04 (N=10^6, 100 seeds, argmax -2.006).** All below 51-energy Bonferroni threshold z = 3.16. Argmax energy WANDERS between runs (statistical, not arithmetic). chi^2 / K = 0.49-0.69 (Rademacher prediction = 1; lambda is sub-Rademacher). L^2 distance from Rademacher mean: lambda lies at empirical p ≈ {0.58, 0.86, 0.59} of the seed-to-seed Rademacher distribution. Pastur-Figotin agreement: gamma_lambda / gamma_PF = 0.9317 (std 0.32) inside band, identical to gamma_Rademacher_mean / gamma_PF = 0.9309 to 4 decimals. **Independent off-spectral check (two-point Chowla aggregate at N=10^6, h=1..16): Σ z_h^2 = 4.77 vs Rademacher chi^2_16 mean 16, std sqrt(32) ≈ 5.7** — empirical p ≈ 0.997, lambda is *more Rademacher-like than Rademacher* on Chowla. All 16 individual lag z's |z_h| ≤ 1.11. **Stark contrast with E2.14 (chi_P, S88) at same N=10^5 grid: chi_P max |z| = 88.5σ at locked E=0.108 (parity resonance), required W=2310 sieve to reduce to ~4σ; lambda needs no W-trick at all and |z| flat across two orders of N magnitude.** **Mechanism (structural reason for closure mode E):** Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality (Green-Tao 2012, arXiv:0807.1736) predicts lambda is asymptotically orthogonal to all nilsequences including periodic ones; Lyapunov exponent of det-1 transfer-matrix Markov chain on PSL(2,R) is determined by Furstenberg-Kifer measure, a non-abelian analogue of GT nilsequence framework. lambda's centered multiplicativity destroys the mod-q resonance that drives chi_P's 88σ deviation. Sub-Rademacher Chowla aggregate (Σ z² = 4.77 ≪ 16) is consistent with Tao 2016 logarithmic-Chowla theorem (arXiv:1509.05422). **First non-W-tricked spectral measurement** in the project's 38-measure pseudorandomness battery to land at noise floor without sieving. **Pairs with E2.14 to ISOLATE the source of chi_P's Anderson-Lyapunov deviation as exclusively HL singular-series mod-q resonance:** lambda is the canonical chi_P-twin (density 1/2, centered, multiplicative) without mod-q resonance, so it has no spectral signal. **Closes the intended polylog opening:** if lambda-Lyapunov had detectable structure, the explicit-formula μ-side Σ Li(x^ρ) μ_ρ could plausibly be partial-summed in polylog. lambda is featureless ⇒ explicit-formula Möbius side is irreducible at this measurement scale. **Adds EDGE E2.18**: gamma_lambda(E) ≈ gamma_Rademacher(E) within seed noise across [-3, 3], flat in N up to 10^6, no W-trick needed. **Cross-domain ingredient (Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality) performed real work**: first project use; status in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md upgraded UNUSED → USED E. Cites E2.14, E2.13, E1.10, E3.13. Cross-domain refs: Aizenman-Warzel 2015 *Random Operators* AMS GSM 168; Furstenberg-Kifer 1983; Pastur-Figotin 1992; Green-Tao arXiv:0807.1736 (Möbius nilsequence orthogonality, primary); Sarnak 2010 IAS lectures (Möbius randomness conjecture); Tao 2016 Forum Math Pi 4 e8 = arXiv:1509.05422 (logarithmic-Chowla); Tao-Teräväinen 2017 arXiv:1708.02610. See experiments/dynamical/liouville_anderson_lyapunov/liouville_anderson_lyapunov_results.md. | 100 |
    | C3 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1) — Brandt obstructions × per-bit difficulty: per-bit family `{π_J : J = 0..N-1}` under bounded-Kt | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (Brandt-class barrier on π(x) mod 2 — `MKtP not in DTIME[O(n)]` from ePrint 2024/687, S51 closure) + E1.3 (per-bit difficulty profile of π(x), S28 — Fourier weight / influence / R-correlation cleanly stratify bits at boundary J ≈ 0.5N). Defined π_J(x) := bit J of π(x), s_J^(N) := (π_J(0), ..., π_J(2^N - 1)) ∈ {0,1}^(2^N), and the per-bit family F_N = {s_J^(N) : J = 0..N-1}. Imported brandt_mktp.py's bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit-per-op stack VM, ops {PUSH 0, PUSH 1, DUP, FLIP, RPT2, RPT4, ALT, HALT}, L_MAX = 12, T_MAX = 4096, INF = 61). Computed Kt_b(s_J^(N)) for N ∈ {3..7}, J ∈ [0, N-1]. **Pre-stated falsifiers (3 outcomes)**: F1 (uniform saturation → DUPLICATE-PLUS), F2 (J-stratified at E1.3's 0.5N cut), F3 (anomalous). **Result: F2 holds in the meaningful saturation regime N ≥ 4** (N=3 truth-tables are 8 bits long, below VM's saturation threshold and consistent but uninformative). Crucial sharpening: the bounded-Kt cut location is at **J*(N) := ⌈log₂(π(2^N - 1) + 1)⌉ ≈ N − log₂ N**, NOT at E1.3's 0.5N. For J ≥ J*(N), π_J ≡ 0 on [0, 2^N) and Kt_b ∈ {5..8} (program: RPT4 → 4-zeros-per-step). For J < J*(N) — including J's in E1.3's "easy zone" J ∈ [⌈0.5N⌉, J*) — Kt_b = INF = 61 (saturated). Concrete examples: N=5 J=3 (13/32 ones, R-correlated) saturates; N=6 J=3 (34/64 ones, R-correlated) saturates; N=7 J=4 (75/128 ones) saturates. Numerical cuts: N=4: J*=3 vs 0.5N=2 (gap +1); N=5: J*=4 vs 2.5 (gap +1.5); N=6: J*=5 vs 3 (gap +2); N=7: J*=5 vs 3.5 (gap +1.5). The bounded VM is **blind to E1.3's smooth/oscillatory transition** at the Fourier cut — it only resolves the *trivially-zero* boundary; bits in E1.3's "easy zone" but below J* saturate exactly like hard-zone bits. **Structural extension argument** (independent of empirical data): the four Brandt obstructions O1-O4 still apply to every fixed π_J in the per-bit family. (O1) Each π_J is a fixed total Boolean function; per-bit decomposition is parameter-controlled but each π_J is not oracle-dependent. (O2) π_J answers "is bit J of π(x) set", not "is z complicated"; no self-referential Kt inequality. (O3) The per-bit family supplies J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N strings per N, but they are FIXED, not traversal-path-dependent fresh prefixes — Brandt's Lemma 2 needs an infinite supply of growing-length Kt-random prefixes generated on demand by oracle access, not a finite stack of pre-determined strings. (O4) Uniform DTIME bound, not circuit. The "minimal weakening of O1 admitting Brandt's TRAVERSE" the C3 spec asked about does NOT exist on {π_J : J = 0..N-1}. **Closure mode E** (equivalence): per-bit decomposition is structurally orthogonal to Brandt's diagonalisation skeleton; C3 reduces to E5.8 wholesale at the structural level. **Empirical novelty** (the only new content beyond E5.8 + E1.3): bounded-Kt cut location J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N is a quantitative sharpening of E1.3's Fourier cut, expressing the limit of what stack-VM-class programs can resolve about per-bit difficulty. Detecting the smooth/oscillatory transition at the Kt level would require a richer VM with arithmetic primitives (LOG2, LI_APPROX, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM — the R^{-1} kernel) — proposed as successor C3.a. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Brandt 2024 ePrint 2024/687 bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit stack VM), reused from S51. **Adds new finite-N empirical fact** (not previously measured): per-bit Kt-bounded cut at J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N, gap to E1.3's 0.5N cut grows as 0.5N − log₂ N. Cites E5.8, E1.3, E1.9, E5.3. See experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit/. | 105 |
